unshare, nsenter: keep the exec securebits in user namespaces

From: Skye Soss <skye@soss.website>

Creating or joining a user namespace resets the securebits to 0,
including EXEC_RESTRICT_FILE, EXEC_DENY_INTERACTIVE, and their locks.
`unshare --user` or `nsenter --user` therefore turns off the checks
done by exec-aware programs, even when the bits were locked.

Save the exec securebits before unshare() and setns(), and set them
again afterwards. These bits can be set without CAP_SETPCAP, so this
works whether or not the process has capabilities in the namespace.
The other securebits are left as the kernel sets them, since restoring
them would change what root can do in the new namespace.

